unlv leading the way in groundbreaking  research making strides toward an  eventual cure for alzheimer's that is  the goal of course right now they're  still working on understanding the  disease and risk factors but as heather  mills shows us they've made a big step  forward  a team of neuroscientists at unlv have  strengthened the connection between  diabetes and alzheimer's essentially  laying the way for more research to  build on this discovery the number of  alzheimer's cases continues to grow  it's estimated to more than triple in  the next 20 to 30 years a startling  number according to the alzheimer's  association 6 million americans are  living with the disease from 2000 to  2019 deaths from alzheimer's increased  145 percent that's why jeff kinney the  chair of unlv's department of brain  health says it's critical to continue  research here inside his lab at unlv two  dozen scientists are working on just  that where we make the best progress is  where  we have several people with different  skill sets and those get fused together  now they're one step closer establishing  a stronger connection between diabetes  and alzheimer's by looking at elevated  blood sugar what we found is that if we  established a good hyperglycemic state  we would start to see changes in the  brain that are consistent with what's  seen  in patients with alzheimer's disease  that's step one a lot of this research  is actually trying to determine  how long someone is hyperglycemic does  it give rise to increasing the risk two  years into a federal grant and working  in tandem with other researchers kinney  says they hope to answer that question  because right now treatments for  alzheimer's disease just delay the  progression we want to solve this  i hope you'll join me next saturday for  the alzheimer's association's walk to  end alzheimer's there's still time to  sign up for yourself or better yet get a  team together it's happening at the las  vegas ballpark and the money raised goes  towards care support and research back  to you
 

